## Actions

1. Read ticket 0029's pinned equivalence pairs, per script class, and consume
   them. Until that corpus exists the check reports `not-run` naming the absent
   corpus as its reason — never `pass`, and never silently absent.
2. Register a check in `bench/acceptance/assertions.py` whose subject verb is
   `query`: index a corpus in which a document is written with form A of a
   pinned pair, ask for form B, assert the document is in the answer. Drive it
   through the ratified verbs only. No source read, no file-layout knowledge,
   no target named.
3. One assertion per MUST clause, per the tracker's standing invariant. The
   promise clause and the exemption clause are two, not one:
   - the promise, asserted on the semantic and hybrid modes;
   - the exemption, asserted on the lexical mode — a target that returns
     nothing for form B on a *lexical* query MUST NOT be scored `fail`, because
     matching the string as typed is what was promised there.
   Without the second arm the first can be satisfied by a target that folds
   everything everywhere, and the exemption becomes untested prose.
4. One row per script class the corpus pins (R7 names the classes). A class the
   corpus does not pin yields **`no test`**, never `pass` — the narrowing the
   ruling accepted is only honest if an unpinned class is visibly unchecked.
5. Wire the layer's existing states rather than inventing one: a target
   declaring `query` absent yields `not-offered`; a run that cannot decide
   yields `not-run` with its reason recorded. Never a green that means the run
   could not look.
6. Once the check exists and has run, add its address to `README.md`'s goal-3
   roster row for R19 beside ticket 0029, and move the standing row's evidence
   token off `inferred` to what actually ran — and not before. `check_progress`
   recomputes the bars and tallies from the tokens; the spelled-out counts near
   the top of that page are hand-maintained and marked with an asterisk, so
   re-verify them in the same edit.

## Test

Red first, and the red must discriminate.

- **The fail-control is a literal matcher.** A stub target that indexes form A
  and answers only byte-identical queries MUST be scored `fail` by the promise
  arm before the check is trusted anywhere. A check that has only ever been run
  against targets that pass has not been shown to report.
- **The exemption's own red.** The same literal matcher, queried in lexical
  mode, MUST be scored `pass` — if both arms move together, the exemption arm
  is not testing the exemption, it is testing the same thing twice.
- **The empty-corpus red.** Run the check against a corpus pinning no pair for
  a script class and confirm the class reads `no test` or `not-run`, never
  `pass`. This is the arm that catches the failure mode the whole layer exists
  to prevent, a green that means nobody looked.

## Verification

- [ ] the promise arm was seen `fail` against the literal-matcher fail-control
      before it was seen `pass` against anything
- [ ] the exemption arm was seen `pass` on the same fail-control in lexical
      mode, and the two arms are demonstrably not one assertion
- [ ] an unpinned script class reads `no test` or `not-run`, and the run says
      which
- [ ] a target declaring `query` absent yields `not-offered`, not `fail`
- [ ] no committed artifact's existing verdict changed silently; any that moves
      is regenerated from the edited code and said so
- [ ] `tests/test_acceptance_layer_is_target_neutral.py` green
- [ ] `make check` green; `./tickets/erg check tickets/` green

## Invariants

- **Interface only.** Every state is reached by driving the target through the
  ratified seven verbs, as a user would. Reading a target's source is what the
  reword took away from this requirement; do not give it back.
- **The layer names no target.** Do not special-case an adapter to make a row
  come out.
- **The fold sweep is not this requirement's evidence and cannot become it.**
  `SPEC.md` §5.2.8 says so in the gate's own entry. Do not couple the two, and
  do not resurrect the retired clause as a second assertion here.
- No threshold, bound or corpus definition is restated in this ticket; `SPEC.md`
  owns R19's clause and ticket 0029 owns the pairs.
- The unit is the MUST clause, not the requirement — inherited from tracker
  ticket 0026.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] a check registered in `bench/acceptance/assertions.py` whose subject is
      the `query` verb decides R19's promise clause, target-neutral, with no
      source access
- [ ] the lexical exemption is a second assertion with its own control, and a
      literal matcher passes it while failing the promise arm
- [ ] the pairs come from ticket 0029's corpus, per script class, and an
      unpinned class is visibly unchecked rather than quietly green
- [ ] every state the layer defines — pass, fail, not-offered, not-run — is
      reachable and was reached at least once in a real run or a control
- [ ] `README.md`'s R19 rows carry this ticket's address and an evidence token
      that names what ran
